  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n     TONIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY...\n\nAn amplified pattern remains over much of the CONUS this afternoon \nwith a deep trough over the Desert Southwest and a strong mid/upper \nridge centered over the western Gulf coast and the mid- \nMississippi River Valley. At the surface, a cold front is\nsagging into northern and western Kansas, but it is beginning to\nslow in its southward progression this afternoon. As the \naforementioned deep layer trough continues it's slow trek \neastward, cloud cover is expected to increase, and eventually, \nscattered showers should begin to develop across the High Plains\nlater this afternoon into this evening. Moisture from the \neastern Pacific will continue to be pulled northward into the \ncentral plains as broad synoptic ascent overspreads much of the \nregion on Sunday. However, much of this forcing will be confined\nto western Kansas and western Nebraska. Therefore, the \nwidespread coverage of showers will generally be limited to \nalong and west of US-281 throughout the day on Sunday. However, \nmoisture will slowly slide eastward by Monday morning, and is \nforecast to linger across the forecast area through Tuesday \nmorning. A couple rounds of showers could occur as far east as \nthe Flint Hills in this timeframe. That being said, rainfall \ntotals through Tuesday morning will not be all that meaningful \nwith less than 0.10\" anticipated for most locations along and \nwest of the Flint Hills. It's possible some locations along US-\n281 see totals up to 0.25\", but instances of this will be \nisolated. Temperature-wise, above average temperatures will \ncontinue through Sunday before a weak cold front arrives on \nMonday. Afternoon highs both Monday and Tuesday will be near \naverage for this time of year with temperatures generally in the\nlow 70s, and overnight lows will be a tick on the mild side, \nremaining in the mid to upper 50s each night.\n\n     WEDNESDAY THROUGH NEXT WEEKEND...\n\nMid to long range guidance remains consistent with showing a series \nof subtle mid/upper troughs traversing across the central plains \nthrough the remainder of next week. This will leave the door open \nfor additional rain chances, especially for Friday and Saturday. \nWith the return of southerly low-level flow after Tuesday, a brief \nwarm up is likely for Wednesday and Thursday, but another weak cold \nfront arriving Friday and Saturday should help to knock temperatures \nback down closer to average for this time of year.",
